{"question": "A 63-year-old man is aiming to improve his health by eating a well balanced diet, walking daily, and quitting smoking following a 45-year smoking history. While on his daily walks he notices a strong cramping pain in his calves that consistently appears after a mile of walking. He sees his physician and a diagnosis of peripheral artery disease with intermittent claudication is made. To improve his symptoms, cilostazol is prescribed. What is the mechanism of action of this medication?", "options": {"A": "Irreversible cyclooxygenase inhibitor", "B": "Glycoprotein IIB/IIIA inhibitor", "C": "Thromboxane synthase inhibitor", "D": "Adenosine diphosphate receptor inhibitor", "E": "Phosphodiesterase inhibitor"}, "pred_answer": "E", "gold_answer": "E", "question_domains": ["Cardiology", "Vascular Medicine", "Pharmacology", "Internal Medicine", "Preventive Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Pharmacology", "Cardiology"], "question_analyses": {"Cardiology": "The scenario presented describes a 63-year-old man who has been diagnosed with peripheral artery disease (PAD) characterized by intermittent claudication. This condition arises from atherosclerosis, where the arteries that supply blood to the limbs become narrowed or blocked, leading to reduced blood flow, particularly during physical activity. The cramping pain in the calves that occurs after walking a certain distance is a classic symptom of intermittent claudication, which is a hallmark of PAD. ### Mechanism of Action of Cilostazol\nCilostazol is a medication commonly used to treat intermittent claudication in patients with PAD. Its mechanism of action involves:\n\n1. **Phosphodiesterase Inhibition**: Cilostazol is a selective inhibitor of phosphodiesterase type 3 (PDE3). By inhibiting this enzyme, cilostazol increases the levels of cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) within platelets and vascular smooth muscle cells. 2. **Vasodilation**: The increase in cAMP leads to vasodilation of the peripheral arteries,